.TH PHASORSTD_SYS 3 "July 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_sys \- Phasor system and runtime functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B include "std/sys.phs";
.PP
.B time()
.B localtime()
.B timef(format)
.B localtimef(format)
.B sleep(milliseconds)
.B sys_os()
.B sys_arch()
.B is_sandboxed()
.B sys_env(name)
.B sys_args()
.B sys_argv(index)
.B sys_argc()
.B sys_get_memory()
.B isatty()
.B wait_for_input()
.B sys_shell(command)
.B sys_fork(program[, args[, pipe[, input[, isolate]]]])
.B sys_fork_detached(program[, args[, pipe[, stream[, isolate]]]])
.B procwait(handle)
.B procstatus(handle)
.B prockill(handle[, signal])
.B procforget(handle)
.B procfree(handle)
.B sys_pid()
.B error(message)
.B reset()
.B shutdown(exit_code)
.fi
.SH TIME FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR time ()
Get a monotonic timestamp in milliseconds since an arbitrary, unspecified reference point
(backed by a steady/monotonic clock). This is
.B not
wall-clock or calendar time \(em it is only meaningful for measuring elapsed durations by
subtracting two calls to \fBtime()\fR. For the current wall-clock time, use \fBlocaltime()\fR
or \fBtimef()\fR/\fBlocaltimef()\fR instead.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Float representing milliseconds since the clock's reference point
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR localtime ()
Get the current wall-clock time, adjusted for the local timezone, as milliseconds since the
Unix epoch.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Float representing local milliseconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, shifted by the local
timezone offset
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR timef (format)
Get the current UTC time formatted according to a format string.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBformat\fR 12
Format string using strftime-style specifiers
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Formatted time string in UTC (not local time; see \fBlocaltimef()\fR for local time), or a
single-space string \fB" "\fR if \fBformat\fR is not a valid format specifier
.PP
.B Format Specifiers:
.RS
.IP \fB%Y\fR 8
Year (4 digits)
.IP \fB%m\fR 8
Month (01-12)
.IP \fB%d\fR 8
Day (01-31)
.IP \fB%H\fR 8
Hour (00-23)
.IP \fB%M\fR 8
Minute (00-59)
.IP \fB%S\fR 8
Second (00-59)
.IP \fB%A\fR 8
Full weekday name
.IP \fB%B\fR 8
Full month name
.RE
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR localtimef (format)
Get the current local time formatted according to a format string. Accepts the same format
specifiers as \fBtimef()\fR, but reflects the local timezone rather than UTC.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBformat\fR 12
Format string using strftime-style specifiers
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Formatted local time string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sleep (milliseconds)
Pause execution for a specified duration.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBmilliseconds\fR 12
Number of milliseconds to sleep (fractional values are allowed)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A single-space string \fB" "\fR (not an empty string)
.RE
.SH SYSTEM INFORMATION
.TP
.BR sys_os ()
Get an integer identifying the current operating system.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer:
.B 0
= Windows,
.B 1
= Linux,
.B 2
= Darwin (macOS),
.B 3
= FreeBSD,
.B 4
= other UNIX-like,
.B 5
= unknown,
.B 6
= running in a sandboxed build.
.PP
The named constants
.BR win32 ", " linux ", " darwin ", " freebsd ", " unix ", " unknown ", and " sandbox
are available as fields on the
.B sys_os
struct once \fBphs/sys.phs\fR (or \fBphs/types/sys.phs\fR) is included..
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_arch ()
Get an integer identifying the current CPU architecture the runtime was built for.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer:
.B 0
= ARM64/AArch64,
.B 1
= 32-bit ARM,
.B 2
= x86-64,
.B 3
= 32-bit x86,
.B 4
= unknown.
.PP
The named constants
.BR arm64 ", " aarch64 ", " arm32 ", " x64 ", " x86_64 ", " x86 ", and " unknown
are available as fields on the
.B sys_arch
struct once \fBphs/sys.phs\fR (or \fBphs/types/sys.phs\fR) is included; the array
.B sys_arch_map
maps a code back to its architecture name.
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR is_sandboxed ()
Check whether the runtime was built as a sandboxed build.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean, true if running in a sandboxed build, false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_get_memory ()
Get the available system memory in bytes.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer bytes of available memory
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR isatty ()
Check whether standard input is connected to an interactive terminal.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Non-zero integer if stdin is a terminal, 0 otherwise
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT AND ARGUMENTS
.TP
.BR sys_env (name)
Get an environment variable value.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBname\fR 12
Environment variable name
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
String value of the environment variable, null on error, false if not found
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_args ()
Get all command-line arguments as an array.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
Array of strings, one per command-line argument, including the program name at index 0.
This is the preferred way to access all arguments; prefer it over calling \fBsys_argv()\fR
in a loop.
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_argv (index)
Get a single command-line argument by index.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBindex\fR 12
Argument index (required; there is no zero-argument form of this function)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
String argument at \fBindex\fR, or null if \fBindex\fR is out of range
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if called with zero arguments
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_argc ()
Get the number of command-line arguments.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer count of arguments (1 being the script/program name)
.RE
.SH CONSOLE FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR wait_for_input ()
Wait for user to press Enter.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Empty string
.RE
.SH PROCESS CONTROL
.TP
.BR sys_shell (command)
Execute a shell command and wait for it to complete.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcommand\fR 12
Shell command to execute
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Result of the underlying system call, typically the command's exit code
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_fork (program[, args[, pipe[, input[, isolate]]]])
Run a native executable as a child process and block until it exits.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBprogram\fR 12
Path to the executable
.IP \fBargs\fR 12
Optional array of string arguments to pass to the process. Must be an array (or null/omitted);
individual variadic string arguments are \fBnot\fR accepted.
.IP \fBpipe\fR 12
Optional boolean (default \fBfalse\fR). If true, the child's stdout is captured and returned;
if \fBinput\fR is also non-empty, it is written to the child's stdin.
.IP \fBinput\fR 12
Optional string (default empty) written to the child's stdin when \fBpipe\fR is true
.IP \fBisolate\fR 12
Optional boolean (default \fBfalse\fR). If true, the child is launched in its own process
group/session rather than sharing the caller's.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A struct with two fields: \fBstatus\fR (integer exit code of the child process) and
\fBoutput\fR (string containing everything the child wrote to stdout if \fBpipe\fR was true,
otherwise null), or null if the process could not be launched
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if more than 5 arguments are given, if \fBprogram\fR is not a string, or if
\fBargs\fR is given but is not an array of strings
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_fork_detached (program[, args[, pipe[, stream[, isolate]]]])
Launch a native executable as a background process without waiting for it to exit.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBprogram\fR 12
Path to the executable
.IP \fBargs\fR 12
Optional array of string arguments to pass to the process. Must be an array (or
null/omitted); individual variadic string arguments are \fBnot\fR accepted.
.IP \fBpipe\fR 12
Optional boolean (default \fBfalse\fR). If true, connect the streams selected by
\fBstream\fR to descriptors the caller can read/write.
.IP \fBstream\fR 12
Optional integer (default \fB1\fR), meaningful only when \fBpipe\fR is true: \fB0\fR wires up
the child's stdin, \fB1\fR its stdout, \fB2\fR its stderr, \fB3\fR both stdout and stderr.
.IP \fBisolate\fR 12
Optional boolean (default \fBfalse\fR), same meaning as in \fBsys_fork()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A struct describing the launched process, or null if it could not be launched:
.RS
.IP \fBpid\fR 12
Integer process ID of the child
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle for use with \fBprocwait()\fR, \fBprocstatus()\fR, \fBprockill()\fR,
\fBprocforget()\fR, and \fBprocfree()\fR
.IP \fBstdin\fR 12
File descriptor (see \fBphasorstd_file\fR(3)) connected to the child's stdin if requested via
\fBstream\fR, otherwise null
.IP \fBstdout\fR 12
File descriptor connected to the child's stdout if requested via \fBstream\fR, otherwise null
.IP \fBstderr\fR 12
File descriptor connected to the child's stderr if requested via \fBstream\fR, otherwise null
.RE
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if more than 5 arguments are given, if \fBprogram\fR is not a string, if
\fBargs\fR is given but is not an array of strings, or if \fBstream\fR is given and is not
0, 1, 2, or 3
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR procwait (handle)
Block until a process launched by \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR exits, then return its exit code.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle from \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer exit code of the process (querying again after the process has already exited returns
the same cached exit code without blocking)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid process handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR procstatus (handle)
Non-blocking check of whether a process launched by \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR has exited.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle from \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer exit code if the process has already exited, or null if it is still running
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid process handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR prockill (handle[, signal])
Terminate a process launched by \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle from \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR
.IP \fBsignal\fR 12
Optional integer signal number (default \fB15\fR, SIGTERM on POSIX). On Windows, a value of
\fB9\fR forcibly terminates the process; any other value sends a \fBCTRL_BREAK_EVENT\fR if the
process was launched with \fBisolate\fR true, or otherwise also forcibly terminates it.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if the signal was sent successfully, false if the process had already exited or
the signal could not be delivered
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid process handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR procforget (handle)
Detach from a process launched by \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR without waiting for it: if it has
already exited its handle is freed immediately, otherwise it is marked to be freed
automatically in the background once it does exit.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle from \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if \fBhandle\fR was a valid process handle, false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR procfree (handle)
Block until a process launched by \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR exits, then free its handle.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
Integer process handle from \fBsys_fork_detached()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if \fBhandle\fR was a valid process handle (whether or not it had already
exited), false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sys_pid ()
Get the process ID of the current process.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer process ID
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR error (message)
Throw a runtime error and reset the VM.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBmessage\fR 12
Error message string
.RE
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error with the provided message
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR reset ()
Reset the virtual machine state.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Null value
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR shutdown (exit_code)
Shutdown the VM and exit the program.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBexit_code\fR 12
Exit code to return to the operating system
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Does not return (exits program)
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Time Operations
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";

// Measure elapsed time (time() is a monotonic clock, not wall-clock time)
var start = time();
// ... do work ...
var elapsed = time() - start;
putf("Elapsed: %f ms", elapsed);

// Current wall-clock time
var now_local_ms = localtime();

// Formatted time
var utc_stamp = timef("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S");       // UTC
var local_stamp = localtimef("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"); // local timezone
puts(utc_stamp);

// Sleep
puts("Waiting...");
sleep(1000);  // Wait 1 second
puts("Done!");
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B System Information
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";

if (sys_os() == sys_os.linux) {
    puts("Running on Linux");
} else if (sys_os() == sys_os.win32) {
    puts("Running on Windows");
}

if (sys_arch() == sys_arch.x64) {
    puts("Running on x86-64");
}

var mem = sys_get_memory();
putf("Available memory: %d bytes", mem);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Environment Variables
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";
include "std/str.phs";

var home = sys_env("HOME");
putf("Home directory: %s", home);

var path = sys_env("PATH");
if (len(path) > 0) {
    puts("PATH is set");
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Command-Line Arguments
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";

var argc = sys_argc();
putf("Argument count: %d", argc);

// Preferred: get every argument as an array
var args = sys_args();
for (var i: i64 = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    puts(args[i]);
}

// Access a single argument by index
if (argc > 1) {
    var first_arg = sys_argv(1);
    putf("First argument: %s", first_arg);
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Console Control
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";
include "std/io.phs"; // clear() lives in the stdio module

clear();  // Clear screen
puts("Screen cleared!");

puts("Press Enter to continue...");
wait_for_input();
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Process Control
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/sys.phs";

// Execute shell command
var result = sys_shell("ls -la");
putf("Command exit code: %d", result);

// Launch a child process and wait; args must be an array
var result1 = sys_fork("/usr/bin/gzip", ["-k", "file.txt"]);
putf("gzip exited with: %d", result1.status);

// Capture stdout by passing pipe=true
var result2 = sys_fork("/usr/bin/echo", ["hello"], true);
puts(result2.output);  // "hello\\n"

// Launch a background process and track it via its handle
var proc = sys_fork_detached("/usr/bin/myservice", ["--daemon"]);
putf("Launched PID %d", proc.pid);

// Poll without blocking
var status = procstatus(proc.handle);
if (status == null) {
    puts("Still running");
}

// Stop it and clean up
prockill(proc.handle);
procfree(proc.handle);

// Get current PID
var pid = sys_pid();
putf("My PID: %d", pid);

// Controlled error
if (invalid_condition) {
    error("Invalid state detected!");
}

// Exit program
if (should_exit) {
    shutdown(0);  // Exit with code 0
}
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.B time()
returns milliseconds from a monotonic clock with an unspecified reference point, as a float;
it is for measuring elapsed durations, not for getting the current date or time
.IP \(bu 2
.B timef()
formats the current time in UTC.
.B localtime()
and
.B localtimef()
use the local system timezone instead
.IP \(bu 2
.B timef()
and
.B localtimef()
return a single-space string \fB" "\fR (on \fBtimef()\fR) or may behave differently (on
\fBlocaltimef()\fR) if given an invalid format specifier; always test format strings during
development
.IP \(bu 2
.B sleep()
returns a single-space string, not an empty string, and is not guaranteed to be precise on
all systems
.IP \(bu 2
.B sys_os()
returns an integer, not a string; compare it against the fields of the \fBsys_os\fR struct
(from \fBphs/types/sys.phs\fR) rather than hardcoding numbers
.IP \(bu 2
.B sys_argv()
requires an explicit index argument and always returns a single value; prefer
.B sys_args()
to retrieve every argument at once
.IP \(bu 2
In sandboxed builds,
.B sys_env()
and
.B sys_args()
may prompt the user for consent before returning a value, and can return null until consent
is granted
.IP \(bu 2
.B clear()
uses ANSI escape codes (may not work in all terminals) and is part of the \fBstdio\fR module
(\fBphs/io.phs\fR), not \fBstdsys\fR
.IP \(bu 2
.BR sys_fork() " returns a struct (" status ", " output "), not a bare integer; " sys_fork_detached()
returns a struct describing the launched process (\fBpid\fR, \fBhandle\fR, \fBstdin\fR,
\fBstdout\fR, \fBstderr\fR), not an exit code
.IP \(bu 2
Every process handle returned by
.B sys_fork_detached()
must eventually be released with exactly one of
.BR procforget() " or " procfree() ,
or its resources are never reclaimed;
.BR procwait() " and " procstatus()
do not release the handle themselves
.IP \(bu 2
.B sys_shell()
runs commands through the system shell
.IP \(bu 2
.B error()
will terminate current execution and reset the VM
.IP \(bu 2
.B shutdown()
immediately exits the program
.IP \(bu 2
Command-line arguments include the program name at index 0
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.B sys_shell()
can run arbitrary shell commands - validate input carefully
.IP \(bu 2
Environment variables may contain sensitive information
